  // same as the standard 22 above.

  const codes = {

    'The Wizard / Secret Knowledge': {
      title: 'The Wizard — The Tension of Protecting Unusual Ability',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuinely rare capability — insight or skill unusual enough that it can meaningfully change a situation when it's actually offered. That capability, once shared rather than guarded, may become real service: ability that gets to do the specific good it was built for. Integrated, this may look like a quiet reputation for being unusually, reliably useful in exactly the moments that call for it. The same rarity that currently feels like something to protect may, once offered, become the thing people remember you for.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever an unusual capability or insight could genuinely help a situation, and the automatic response under pressure is to hold it back rather than offer it. This may repeat as a pattern of real, sometimes rare ability that stays quietly guarded, even in contexts where sharing it would clearly serve the people involved. Over time, that pattern can leave real value sitting unused, known to no one but you. The rarer the ability, the more isolating it can feel to keep it permanently out of view.`,
      invitation: `Offer the ability or insight in one specific, lower-stakes situation this week. This may shift by noticing whether the anticipated risk of exposure actually materializes, and letting that real outcome replace the old assumption. You are allowed to be useful in the exact way you're unusual. Where could your guarded ability quietly help someone this week?`,
    },

    'Wasted Talent': {
      title: 'Wasted Talent — The Tension of Capability Left Undeveloped',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuinely strong talent — real capability that, when actually tested at full effort, may prove itself to be exactly as good as it's always quietly suspected of being. That talent, once tried openly rather than protected, may become a body of finished, shared work rather than a private ceiling that's never actually been reached. Integrated, this may look like a track record of honest attempts, some of which fall short and none of which cost you the talent itself. The same capability that currently stays close to but never quite at its ceiling may, once tested, discover the ceiling was higher than expected.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever real talent is close to being tested publicly — a piece of work ready to be shared, a skill ready to be used at its actual level. The automatic response under pressure can be to hold back, protecting the talent from the risk of falling short in view of other people. This may repeat as a pattern of near-completions, almost-finished work, and capability that stays close to but never quite at its actual ceiling. Over time, that pattern can leave real talent permanently undiscovered, including by you.`,
      invitation: `Complete and share one piece of work at its current, honest stage. This may shift by resisting the urge to hold it back until it feels fully ready, and letting the actual response, whatever it is, replace the imagined one. You are allowed to be tested and found human. What would you attempt this month if falling short were survivable?`,
    },

    'The Prisoner': {
      title: 'The Prisoner — The Tension of an Old Sense of Confinement',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine survival instinct — real, hard-won awareness of limits that once mattered enormously and kept you safe. That awareness, once tested against current, actual circumstances, may become genuine freedom: the ability to tell which limitations are still real and which have simply outlived their original purpose. Integrated, this may look like a life with far more room in it than the old instinct assumed, discovered simply by pressing against the walls that were never actually load-bearing. The same instinct that currently assumes confinement may, once tested, discover most of the doors quietly open.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate around situations that involve choice or freedom, where the automatic response is to assume the limitation is fixed rather than to test whether it still holds. This may repeat as a persistent sense of restriction in some specific area of life that doesn't always match the actual, current circumstances. Over time, that pattern can mean years spent inside a boundary that was never actually re-checked. The old limit can feel so familiar that testing it seems riskier than simply staying inside it.`,
      invitation: `Identify one specific area where a limitation is assumed rather than confirmed. This may shift by taking one concrete step to test whether it's actually still there, and letting the real result, not the old assumption, decide what happens next. You are allowed to test the walls. Which limit in your life have you never actually pressed against?`,
    },

    'World of Passions / Temptation': {
      title: 'World of Passions — The Tension of Appetite Outrunning Judgment',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for aliveness — real, vivid appetite and desire that most people spend their lives trying to reclaim more of, not less. That capacity, once paired with real discernment, may become the ability to want intensely and still choose wisely, with neither quality diminishing the other. Integrated, this may look like a life that's both fully felt and genuinely well-judged, desire and discretion working together instead of racing each other. The same intensity that currently outruns judgment may, once slowed by a single deliberate pause, become one of your most reliable strengths.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate around situations involving strong desire or stimulation, where the automatic response under pressure is to act on the pull before pausing to weigh its actual cost. This may repeat as a pattern of decisions that felt irresistible in the moment and, in hindsight, cost more than they seemed to at the time. Over time, that pattern can leave a trail of costs that a single pause would likely have prevented. The intensity of the pull itself can be mistaken for proof that the choice is correct.`,
      invitation: `Build in one deliberate pause between the pull toward something intense and the decision to act on it. This may shift by weighing the actual cost during that pause, long enough for judgment to actually catch up with appetite. You are allowed to want intensely and still choose slowly. What does the pause cost you, really, compared to what the rush has?`,
    },

    'Family Betrayal': {
      title: 'Betrayal of Family — The Tension of Pride Over Loyalty',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine drive for advancement — real ambition and self-respect that push you to grow beyond where you started, including beyond your family of origin. That drive, once it no longer requires distance from family to feel legitimate, may become the ability to hold both loyalty and personal growth at once. Integrated, this may look like a relationship to family that's warm rather than strained, with pride finally freed from needing to compete with belonging. The same ambition that currently reads as distance may, once examined, coexist easily with real closeness.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate specifically within family or origin-based relationships, where the automatic response under pressure is to protect pride or position rather than to stay loyal to the people with a legitimate claim on your care. This may repeat as a pattern of distance or friction in family contexts specifically, even when the same difficulty is handled easily elsewhere. Over time, that pattern can leave family relationships carrying a strain no other relationship in your life quite has. The pride that feels necessary to protect in these moments is often protecting something smaller than it seems.`,
      invitation: `Choose one specific family relationship and practice an act of humility there directly — an acknowledgment, an apology, a genuine ask for repair. This may shift by letting pride step aside for one honest gesture, rather than continuing to hold the distance in place. You are allowed to repair without losing face. What would you say to them if pride weren't in the room?`,
    },

    'Sorcerer, Hermit, Rejection of Knowledge': {
      title: 'The Guarded Hermit — The Tension of Trusting No One With What You Carry',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine depth of inner life — real perception and knowing, developed enough that other people already sense there's more to you than what's shown. That depth, once offered rather than guarded, may become real intimacy: closeness built on being actually known rather than carefully managed. Integrated, this may look like relationships where you're finally reached as fully as you already reach other people. The same depth that currently stays one careful step removed may, once opened, become the very thing that deepens your closest relationships.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever trust is on the table — sharing something real with another person, being vulnerable about what you actually know or sense. The automatic response under pressure can be caution first, warmth second: a guardedness that keeps real intimacy one careful step removed, even in relationships that have otherwise earned it. This may repeat as a pattern of being the one others confide in while remaining, by comparison, genuinely hard to reach. Over time, that asymmetry can leave you well-trusted but rarely truly known.`,
      invitation: `Practice one small, deliberate act of unguarded honesty with someone already safe. This may shift by sharing something real before it feels fully ready to be shared, and noticing that the closeness which follows outweighs the old risk. You are allowed to be reached. Who has already earned a door you haven't opened?`,
    },

    'Magical Sacrifice': {
      title: 'The Watchful Influence — The Tension of Power That Still Needs Checking',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for real influence — the ability to shape a negotiation, a piece of guidance, or a relationship for the better because your read of things genuinely carries weight. That capacity, once paired with honest, ongoing self-examination, may become influence that's actually trustworthy, used deliberately rather than swinging between overreach and total avoidance. Integrated, this may look like a settled relationship to your own power, exercised with intention rather than either fear or excess. The same influence that currently swings between extremes may, once examined regularly, become something you can simply trust yourself with.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate around any situation where personal influence could shape an outcome — a negotiation, guidance someone is relying on, a relationship where one person's read of things quietly carries more weight. The automatic response under pressure can swing between two extremes: reaching for more influence than the moment actually calls for, or overcorrecting into refusing to use it at all, even when it would genuinely help. This may repeat as a pull toward spiritual or esoteric interests alongside a real wariness of them — an uncertainty about whether your own motives are fully clean. That uncertainty, left unexamined, can push the swing further toward one extreme or the other rather than settling it.`,
      invitation: `Name your actual motive to yourself before acting on any real influence you hold. This may shift through a small, regular practice of that honest pause, letting it be enough to keep the use of influence clean either way. You are allowed to hold influence without fearing your own hands. What motive, named honestly, would make your next act of influence feel clean?`,
    },

    'Love Magic': {
      title: 'Love Magic — The Tension of Earning Love Through Self-Sacrifice',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for devotion — real, deep willingness to invest in the people you love. That capacity, once it no longer needs to earn its place through self-erasure, may become love that's mutual rather than transactional, freely given because it's freely received in return. Integrated, this may look like relationships where your needs are stated plainly and met, rather than quietly traded for through more giving. The same devotion that currently overextends to earn love may, once tested, discover it was already lovable without the overextending.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate anywhere love or approval feels uncertain, where the automatic response is to give more — more accommodation, more self-erasure, more compensating — rather than to simply ask whether the connection is actually mutual. This may repeat as relationships that require increasingly excessive giving to sustain, alongside a persistent inner emptiness that more giving never quite fills. Over time, that pattern can leave you exhausted in relationships that were never actually asking this much of you. The emptiness itself can be mistaken as proof that you simply haven't given enough yet.`,
      invitation: `Name one specific need directly to someone this week, instead of trying to earn its fulfillment through further accommodation. This may shift by noticing what happens when the need is simply stated, plainly, without the usual compensating effort attached. You are allowed to be loved without earning it. What need would you name tonight if you trusted it wouldn't cost you the relationship?`,
    },

    'Rebel': {
      title: 'The Rebel — The Tension of Opposing on Reflex',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for independence — real, hard-won autonomy and a healthy resistance to being controlled by expectations that were never actually yours. That capacity, once separated from reflexive opposition, may become chosen closeness: connection that's entered into freely, precisely because it's no longer confused with surrender. Integrated, this may look like relationships you're genuinely part of, not because you stopped being independent, but because independence and belonging finally stopped competing. The same instinct that currently opposes on reflex may, once examined, discover most closeness was never actually a threat to it.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate specifically around family or authority, where the automatic response is opposition itself — pushing back, distancing, rejecting the expectation — regardless of whether the actual expectation was reasonable. This may repeat as a pattern of feeling like an outsider even in close relationships, with real connection kept at a careful, self-protective distance. Over time, that pattern can leave you isolated from people who were never actually trying to control you. The reflex to oppose can trigger before the actual expectation has even been evaluated.`,
      invitation: `Choose one relationship currently held at a distance and offer one small, direct gesture of reconnection. This may shift by not waiting for the other person to prove they deserve it first, and letting the gesture stand on its own. You are allowed to belong without surrendering. Where has opposition become a habit rather than a choice?`,
    },

    'Physical Suffering': {
      title: 'The Guarded Body — The Tension of Bracing Against Pain That Hasn’t Arrived',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine attentiveness to the body — real, careful awareness of health and physical vulnerability that, once steadied, can genuinely protect you and the people you love. That attentiveness, once it settles into ordinary, consistent care instead of extremes, may become real trust in the body as something reliable rather than something to constantly monitor or ignore. Integrated, this may look like a calm, sustainable relationship to physical wellbeing, neither hypervigilant nor avoidant. The same attentiveness that currently swings between extremes may, once steadied, become quiet, dependable care.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate around anything involving the body — your own health, or the health of someone vulnerable and close to you — where the automatic response under pressure is either hypervigilance or complete avoidance, rarely anything calmer in between. This may repeat as a low tolerance for ordinary discomfort, physical or emotional, and outsized alarm at minor health scares involving people you love. Over time, that swing can cost real peace of mind on both ends of it. Neither extreme actually gives the body the steady attention it's genuinely asking for.`,
      invitation: `Choose one consistent, moderate act of physical self-care this week. This may shift by practicing staying with it calmly rather than swinging toward vigilance or neglect, and letting ordinary attention be genuinely enough. You are allowed to treat your body as a companion, not a case. What would ordinary, calm care look like this week?`,
    },

    'Warrior': {
      title: 'The Warrior — The Tension of Meeting Every Challenge as Combat',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for leadership — real courage and decisiveness in the face of conflict that most people would rather avoid. That capacity, once it no longer requires winning to feel valid, may become leadership built on trust: influence that holds because people choose to follow it, not because they've been outmaneuvered. Integrated, this may look like a real gift for navigating disagreement toward a shared outcome rather than a personal victory. The same courage that currently treats everything as combat may, once redirected, become genuinely collaborative strength.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate in any situation involving disagreement or competition, where the automatic response is to treat it as something to win rather than something to navigate. This may repeat as relationships and collaborations that become unnecessarily adversarial, alongside a genuine leadership capacity that struggles to find a constructive outlet. Over time, that pattern can cost real trust in relationships that never actually needed to be contests. The instinct to win can convert an ordinary disagreement into a battle before anyone's actually decided it should be one.`,
      invitation: `Enter one upcoming disagreement with the explicit intention of finding a shared solution rather than a winner. This may shift by noticing whether the old urge to dominate the outcome softens once winning is no longer the goal. You are allowed to lay the sword down and still be strong. What might you win by not needing to?`,
    },

    'The Solitary Woman': {
      title: 'The Solitary Woman — The Tension of Staying Just Out of Reach',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for self-protection — real, well-earned instinct that has kept you steady through real risk of loss. That instinct, once it allows real closeness alongside it, may become the ability to stay open even when loss remains a possibility, because openness itself is understood to be worth the risk. Integrated, this may look like relationships that go as deep as they're capable of going, rather than stopping short at a survivable distance. The same instinct that currently keeps relationships at a careful distance may, once tested, discover real closeness is worth what it costs.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate as a relationship starts to deepen, where the automatic response is to keep it at a survivable distance — pleasant, steady, but not fully open — rather than risk the kind of closeness that could actually be lost. This may repeat as relationships that look stable from the outside while remaining quietly guarded underneath. Over time, that guardedness can become its own quiet, ongoing loss. The distance kept to avoid one kind of loss can end up costing exactly the closeness it was meant to protect.`,
      invitation: `Allow one relationship currently kept at a comfortable distance to move one step closer than feels entirely safe. This may shift by noticing what actually happens, rather than what the old fear predicted. You are allowed to be close enough to lose. If the guard came down one inch, who would you let nearer?`,
    },

    'The Unborn Child': {
      title: 'The Unborn Child — The Tension of Withholding Warmth From Fear of Loss',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for tenderness — real, deep care for family and continuity that runs quieter than it should, protected out of an old fear of loss. That tenderness, once offered without rationing, may become the kind of warmth that actually reaches the people it's meant for, unguarded and fully felt. Integrated, this may look like closeness with family that finally matches the depth of care that's always been there underneath. The same tenderness that currently holds part of itself back may, once released, become the thing your family remembers most about you.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate around children, family continuity, or a lingering coolness toward a parent, where the automatic response is quiet guardedness rather than open affection — as though warmth given fully might somehow be taken away. This may repeat as overprotectiveness toward the people you love most, or a persistent difficulty feeling fully at ease around family. Over time, that guardedness can ration exactly the warmth it was meant to protect. The people closest to you may sense the held-back quality even when the reason for it is never named.`,
      invitation: `Offer one deliberate, unguarded act of warmth this week, toward a child, a parent, or yourself. This may shift by resisting the usual instinct to hold part of it back, and letting the warmth land fully instead. You are allowed to love at full warmth now. What affection are you rationing against a loss that hasn't come?`,
    },

    'The Oppressed Soul': {
      title: 'The Oppressed Soul — The Tension of Waiting for Permission',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for humility and consideration — real respect for other people's perspectives that keeps you from acting rashly. That capacity, once paired with real trust in your own judgment, may become the ability to weigh other views and still decide for yourself. Integrated, this may look like a life increasingly built around your own preferences and ambitions, informed by others but no longer dependent on their sign-off. The same consideration that currently defers automatically may, once balanced, become genuine discernment rather than permission-seeking.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate at any real decision point, where the automatic response is to defer — to wait for someone else's approval or direction rather than trusting your own judgment. This may repeat as a pattern of living inside expectations set by other people, with your own preferences and ambitions quietly kept in the background. Over time, that pattern can leave a whole life shaped more by permission than by preference. The habit of waiting for a sign-off can persist long after anyone else was actually asking you to wait.`,
      invitation: `Make one real decision this week entirely on your own terms. This may shift by letting it stand without seeking outside confirmation afterward, and noticing that the decision holds fine without it. You are allowed to be your own permission. What decision is already made in you, waiting only for a sign-off that was never required?`,
    },

    'The Emperor': {
      title: 'The Emperor — The Tension of Authority Without Balance',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for authority — real ability to hold responsibility and direct a situation when it's actually needed. That capacity, once steadied between the two extremes, may become authority that's genuinely balanced: present without dominating, available without disappearing. Integrated, this may look like a settled relationship to your own power, neither gripping it too tightly nor abandoning it altogether. The same instinct that currently swings between over-control and checking out may, once steadied, become reliable, quiet leadership.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate around authority itself — your own, or a father figure's — where the automatic response swings between over-controlling everyone nearby and checking out of responsibility entirely, rarely landing in between. This may repeat as recurring friction in relationships with paternal figures, and a similar instability in how you hold power in your own life. Over time, that instability can cost trust in both directions — from the people you over-control and the people you abandon. The swing between extremes can feel like adapting to circumstances, when it's often the same unresolved tension surfacing twice.`,
      invitation: `Choose one area where you currently either over-control or avoid responsibility. This may shift by consciously practicing the steadier middle version instead, just once, to see how it feels, and by allowing forgiveness toward an imperfect father figure to free up energy currently spent managing that old friction. You are allowed to hold power gently. Where could steadiness replace either the grip or the absence?`,
    },

    'The Spiritual Priest': {
      title: 'The Spiritual Priest — The Tension of Doubting a Knowing That Runs Deep',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine depth of spiritual or intuitive insight — real perception that runs deeper than most people ever develop. That insight, once trusted enough to be spoken, may become a genuine gift to other people: guidance or teaching that only exists once it's actually voiced. Integrated, this may look like private clarity that finally becomes public voice, offered before it's provably "right," because it's true enough to be useful as it stands. The same knowing that currently gets discounted before anyone else can may, once spoken, become the thing people come to you for.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever your own spiritual or intuitive insight asks to be trusted publicly — teaching, guiding, or simply naming what you sense is true. The automatic response under pressure can be retreat: quietly discounting your own perception before anyone else gets the chance to, and letting the moment pass to someone with less to lose. This may repeat as private clarity that rarely becomes public voice, no matter how consistently accurate it proves to be. Each unspoken instance can quietly reinforce the belief that the knowing was never trustworthy in the first place.`,
      invitation: `Name one thing you already sense is true, out loud, to one person, before it feels fully ready to be said. This may shift by letting the insight stand on its own merit rather than waiting for proof that may never come. You are allowed to speak before you can prove it. What knowing have you been discounting on everyone else's behalf?`,
    },

    'Disappointment of the Lineage': {
      title: 'Disappointment of the Lineage — The Tension of Working Without Credit',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for real, sustained effort — the ability to keep working at something even when recognition is slow or absent. That capacity, once no longer dependent on outside credit, may become genuine vocation: work pursued for its own value, which turns out to be a steadier source of worth than praise ever was. Integrated, this may look like a relationship to your own effort that no longer waits on anyone else's applause to feel worthwhile. The same persistence that currently waits for recognition may, once redirected inward, become the thing that finally restores your sense of worth.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate specifically around recognition, where genuine effort keeps meeting delay, being overlooked, or landing as someone else's success. The automatic response under pressure can be quiet resentment toward family, paired with a persistent hunger for praise that doesn't quite ease even when it arrives. This may repeat as obstacles that feel disproportionate to the actual effort involved. Over time, that pattern can leave real work feeling hollow even when it succeeds.`,
      invitation: `Commit to one activity that genuinely inspires you, independent of whether it's noticed or credited by anyone else. This may shift by continuing it regardless of recognition, and letting the doing of it be the actual reward. You are allowed to work for the love of the work. What would you keep doing if no one ever clapped?`,
    },

    'The Overseer': {
      title: 'The Overseer — The Tension of Managing What Isn’t Yours to Manage',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for care — real attentiveness to when someone nearby is struggling, and a strong instinct to help. That attentiveness, once it waits to be invited, may become real support: help that lands as care rather than supervision, because it was actually asked for. Integrated, this may look like relationships where people feel accompanied by you rather than managed by you. The same instinct that currently steps in unasked may, once it learns to wait, become the kind of support people actually seek out.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever someone nearby is struggling, where the automatic response is to step in and manage the situation — offering direction, oversight, or unsolicited help — rather than waiting to be asked. This may repeat as strained relationships where people feel supervised rather than supported, even when the intention behind it was genuinely caring. Over time, that pattern can push away exactly the closeness the caring was meant to build. Good intentions rarely soften how managed the other person actually feels in the moment.`,
      invitation: `Identify one relationship where you habitually manage or oversee. This may shift by consciously waiting to be asked before offering input, just this once, and noticing what the other person does with the room you've made. You are allowed to trust people with their own struggles. Who might grow if you stepped back one pace?`,
    },

    'Pride': {
      title: 'Pride — The Tension of Charm Curdling Into Superiority',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine gift for presence — real charm, attractiveness, and influence over a room that draws people toward you naturally. That gift, once it no longer requires anyone else to feel smaller, may become genuine magnetism: the kind that makes people feel more themselves around you, not less. Integrated, this may look like relationships that deepen rather than cool once admiration alone stops being the currency between you. The same magnetism that currently curdles into superiority may, once tempered with real curiosity about others, become something far more durable than admiration.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever charm, attractiveness, or influence over a room comes easily, where the automatic response is a quiet sense of being above the people who don't have it — a superiority that can slide into contempt without quite meaning to. This may repeat as relationships that cool once admiration stops being offered, and a discomfort with any environment where you're not the most noticed person in it. Over time, that pattern can leave you surrounded by admirers and genuinely known by very few. The ease of drawing admiration can make it easy to mistake it for genuine closeness.`,
      invitation: `Spend real time with someone you'd normally dismiss as unimpressive. This may shift by practicing genuine curiosity about them instead of quiet evaluation, and noticing what that curiosity actually gives you. You are allowed to be one among many and still be luminous. When did being admired start standing in for being known?`,
    },

    'Destruction and Death': {
      title: 'The Reckoning — The Tension of Disregarding the Human Cost',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for ambition — real drive toward power, control, or a goal, strong enough to push through obstacles that stop other people. That drive, once it includes the human cost as part of the calculation, may become ambition that's actually sustainable: goals pursued in ways you can genuinely live with afterward. Integrated, this may look like a life oriented as much toward protecting or improving the people it passes through as toward the goal itself. The same drive that currently discounts human cost may, once it accounts for it, become far more powerful, not less.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate around any pursuit of power, control, or a goal, where the automatic response under pressure is to discount the cost to the people affected by it, treating outcomes as separate from the harm required to reach them. This may repeat as a pull toward high-stakes or high-consequence situations, paired with real discomfort when the human cost becomes impossible to ignore. Over time, that pattern can cost relationships and trust the goal itself never actually required losing. The discomfort that finally surfaces is often the accurate signal that got overridden much earlier.`,
      invitation: `Choose one current pursuit and name, honestly, what or who its cost has been so far. This may shift by adjusting the approach accordingly, once the actual cost is finally in view. You are allowed to win in ways you can live with. What has this pursuit already cost that you haven't counted?`,
    },

    'Physical Aggression': {
      title: 'The Coiled Response — The Tension of Meeting Stress With Force',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for intensity — real, powerful energy that, once it's not funneled straight into aggression, may become forceful advocacy: the ability to meet a difficult moment with real strength rather than either explosion or suppression. That capacity, once it includes a pause before the surge, may become a presence people experience as powerful rather than frightening. Integrated, this may look like real strength that stays level under provocation, precisely because the older pain underneath it has finally been acknowledged. The same intensity that currently surges toward force may, once examined, become steadiness that still carries real weight.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate under stress or provocation, where the automatic response is a surge toward aggression — an outburst, an intimidating edge, a struggle to stay level — before any calmer option gets a chance to register. This may repeat as strained relationships around conflict, and a body that carries old tension in ways that surface later as physical strain. Over time, that pattern can cost relationships and health in equal measure. The speed of the surge can make it feel involuntary, even in moments it was actually still possible to interrupt.`,
      invitation: `Notice, in the moment stress first spikes, the physical warning signs that usually precede the reaction. This may shift by using one grounding practice deliberately before responding, giving the calmer option a real chance to register. You are allowed to feel the surge and choose the softer move. What older pain does the force keep standing guard over?`,
    },

    'The Dark Magician': {
      title: 'The Dark Magician — The Tension of Influence Without Consent',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine gift for persuasion — real charisma and insight, capable of moving a situation in a direction that serves everyone involved when it's actually invited. That gift, once it waits for consent, may become influence that builds trust rather than eroding it, because it was never exercised on someone who hadn't asked for it. Integrated, this may look like relationships that are charged and compelling precisely because they were built on mutual choice, not persuasion. The same charisma that currently sways without invitation may, once it waits to be asked, become genuinely trusted rather than merely effective.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever personal charisma or insight could sway a situation in your favor, where the automatic response is to use it regardless of whether the other person actually invited the influence. This may repeat as relationships that feel unbalanced in hindsight — charged and compelling in the moment, but built on persuasion rather than mutual choice. Over time, that pattern can leave relationships that look intense from the outside and feel hollow from within. The persuasive skill itself can make it genuinely hard to tell where influence ends and someone's own choice begins.`,
      invitation: `Wait to be asked before offering guidance or persuasion in one relationship this week, even when you're confident you're right. This may shift by noticing what changes in the relationship once your influence waits for an invitation. You are allowed to be compelling without steering. What changes when your influence waits for an invitation?`,
    },

    'The Sacrificed Soul': {
      title: 'The Sacrificed Soul — The Tension of Asking Without Words',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for restraint — real consideration for other people that keeps you from demanding attention you're not certain you deserve. That capacity, once paired with the willingness to actually name a need, may become mature, direct communication: needs stated plainly and met more fully than any indirect signal ever managed. Integrated, this may look like relationships where care flows toward you because it was asked for, not because it was signaled through withdrawal. The same restraint that currently expresses itself through indirect signals may, once it finds words, become a genuinely clear and honest voice.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever a real need for care or attention arises, where the automatic response is to signal it indirectly — through withdrawal, minor crises, or quiet self-neglect — rather than naming the need outright. This may repeat as people around you responding generously to the indirect signal while the actual need underneath goes unspoken and only partly met. Over time, that pattern can leave real needs chronically under-met, even by people who genuinely want to help. The indirect signal can get answered just enough to keep the direct request feeling unnecessary.`,
      invitation: `Name one specific need in plain words to someone close, instead of waiting for it to be noticed. This may shift by letting the plain request stand on its own, without the usual indirect signal attached. You are allowed to ask plainly and be answered. What need have you been translating into signals instead of words?`,
    },

    'The Warrior of Faith': {
      title: 'The Warrior of Faith — The Tension of Certainty That Leaves No Room',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for conviction — real, strong belief that gives you a stable center other people can feel and often rely on. That capacity, once it includes real curiosity about differing views, may become influence through example rather than insistence — conviction that draws people in rather than pushing them toward agreement. Integrated, this may look like a social world that stays genuinely varied, held together by respect rather than consensus. The same conviction that currently presses its point may, once it makes room for other views, become far more persuasive than insistence ever was.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever your own convictions meet a differing view, where the automatic response is to press the point rather than genuinely consider the other perspective. This may repeat as relationships strained by an insistence on being right, and a social circle that quietly narrows toward people who already agree. Over time, that narrowing can cost you exactly the perspectives that would have tested and strengthened your own. Conviction this strong can feel like clarity even in the moments it's actually closing off real information.`,
      invitation: `Listen fully to one differing view this week without arguing back, simply to understand it rather than to respond to it. This may shift by noticing what the differing view actually protects you from missing. You are allowed to be certain and still curious. What might a differing view protect you from missing?`,
    },

    'Self-Destruction': {
      title: 'The Quiet Surrender — The Tension of Giving Up Before Trying the Next Step',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for honesty about difficulty — real willingness to feel the full weight of a hard chapter rather than minimizing it. That capacity, once paired with real self-care through the difficulty, may become genuine resilience: the ability to hold a hard truth and still keep tending to yourself through it. Integrated, this may look like a track record of reaching the other side of difficult chapters, not by denying how hard they were, but by caring for yourself all the way through them. The same honesty that currently reads as hopelessness may, once paired with self-care, become the foundation real recovery is built on.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate under real hardship, where the automatic response is to treat the difficulty as confirmation that things are hopeless, rather than as one obstacle among others that might still be worked through. This may repeat as a pattern of neglecting basic self-care exactly when it matters most, and a persistent difficulty recognizing your own worth independent of how things are currently going. Over time, that pattern can make a hard chapter even harder than it needed to be. The hopelessness can feel like an accurate read of the facts rather than what it actually is, a symptom of the hardship itself.`,
      invitation: `Choose one small, concrete act of self-care today. This may shift by naming the difficulty out loud to one trusted person rather than carrying it alone, and letting that shared weight be genuinely lighter. You are allowed to matter on your worst day. What small kindness could you offer yourself before tonight?`,
    },

    'The Dictator': {
      title: 'The Dictator — The Tension of a Confident Mask Over Real Doubt',
      mastery: `Underneath this tension may sit a genuine capacity for composure — real ability to hold a steady, capable front in situations that would rattle other people. That capacity, once it makes room for visible uncertainty, may become trust that runs deeper than the polished version ever earned, because people finally see the whole picture. Integrated, this may look like relationships and leadership built on a mix of real confidence and real honesty, rather than confidence alone. The same composure that currently covers doubt may, once it lets the doubt show, become something people trust even more.`,
      shadow: `This placement may activate whenever real self-doubt surfaces, where the automatic response is to cover it with an outward show of confidence and control rather than let anyone see the uncertainty underneath. This may repeat as a pattern of swinging between over-functioning for others and quietly resenting the imbalance, with the genuine doubt underneath rarely getting spoken aloud. Over time, that pattern can leave you feeling fundamentally unknown, even by people close to you. The mask can end up working so well that even you start to lose track of what's underneath it.`,
      invitation: `Share one genuine doubt with someone trustworthy this week, rather than resolving it privately behind a confident front. This may shift by noticing whether the shared doubt actually costs you the respect the old fear predicted. You are allowed to be unsure out loud. What doubt, spoken, might bring you closer instead of costing you respect?`,
    },

  };

